1. What are Profile Files?

Profile files are configuration files that automatically run when a user logs in or opens a shell.

These files are used to:

Set environment variables
Configure the shell
Create aliases
Set the PATH variable

2. Real-Life Example

Imagine you join a company every morning.

As soon as you enter:

Your office computer turns on.
Outlook opens.
Teams starts automatically.
Your printer is connected.

You didn't start these manually—they happen automatically.

Similarly, when you log in to Linux, profile files automatically configure your environment.

3. Why Do We Need Profile Files?

Instead of typing these commands every time:

export PROJECT=Linux
alias ll='ls -l'
cd /home/bilal/projects

you can save them in a profile file.

Whenever you log in, Linux runs these commands automatically.

4. Understanding Each Profile File
a) /etc/profile

This file contains settings for all users.

Whenever any user logs in, Linux executes this file.

Example:

sudo vi /etc/profile

Example content:

export COMPANY=ABC

Now every user gets:

echo $COMPANY

Output

ABC

b) ~/.profile

This file is only for one user.

Example:

vi ~/.profile

Add

export PROJECT=Linux

Save the file.

Logout and login again.

Check

echo $PROJECT

Output

Linux

Only that user sees this variable.

c) ~/.bash_profile

Used for login Bash shells, mainly on RHEL-based systems.

Example

vi ~/.bash_profile

Add

export NAME=Bilal

Logout and login.

Check

echo $NAME

d) ~/.bashrc

This file is executed whenever you open a new interactive Bash terminal.

It is commonly used for:

Aliases
Functions
Shell prompt customization

Example

vi ~/.bashrc

Add

alias ll='ls -l'

Save.

Reload

source ~/.bashrc

Now

ll

works like

ls -l

e) ~/.bash_logout

Runs automatically when you log out.

Example

vi ~/.bash_logout

Add

echo "Good Bye!"

When you log out

Output

Good Bye!

5. Login Shell vs Non-Login (Interactive) Shell

This is important for interviews.

a) Login Shell

Started when you:

Log in to Linux
Log in through SSH

Reads files such as:

/etc/profile

then one of:

~/.bash_profile

or

~/.profile

b) Interactive Non-Login Shell

Started when you open another terminal window after logging in.

It reads:

~/.bashrc
